new state gun legislation is headed to  the desk of Florida Governor Rick Scott  this morning after last month's deadly  school shooting in parkland he has not  said if he will sign it Florida  lawmakers voted for final approval of  the bill last night it raises the age to  buy rifles increases mental health  resources and allows some teachers to be  armed the accused gunman was formally  charged yesterday with 17 counts of  first degree murder and 17 counts of  attempted murder Adriana Diaz is at the  State Capitol in Tallahassee Adriana  good morning good morning the state  representative who introduced this  measure in the house as it pushed both  Republicans and Democrats beyond their  comfort level the families of all 17  victims supported the bill and they  wrote this letter to lawmakers to tell  them so 67 days 15 days mr. speaker so  the bill passes read the next bill  lawmakers say they kept their promise  passing legislation in the name of the  17 people who died at Marjory Stoneman  Douglas High School Andrew Pollock's 18  year old daughter meadow was killed in  the shooting it's important for the  country to unite in the same way the 17  families united in support of this bill  Republican state representative Jose  Oliva introduced the measure in the  house I know that some of you think that  this is gonna put guns in schools and  kill children I would not be standing  here if I thought that that was the case  the bill would limit rifle sales to  people 21 or over after a three day  waiting period allocate 400 million  dollars for provisions like school  mental health programs and allow some  teachers to be armed the question of  arming school staff has fueled days of  debate a black a brown boy who may be  running down the hall to get away like  everyone else who reaches for his cell  phone to call his parents may be seen by  the Guardian not as a student but as the  shooter a lot of the students have  actually came at this state house have  called for a ban on assault rifles and  for teachers not to be armed or staff  members not to be armed this bill does  neither of those things  we've met with many students and parents  and  including the parents of the 17 that  were slain all of them have signed a  letter urging everyone to support this  legislation only teachers who don't  teach full-time can be armed so like a  teacher who also coaches and that's only  in the counties that allow it the bill  now goes to the desk of Republican  Governor Rick Scott who opposes arming  teachers  he says he'll read it line by line and  consult with victims families before  deciding whether or not to sign it  gale a lot of people watching that very  closely thank you very much Adriana
 

